Ranking Rural Road Upgrades from Access Sequences Overview This is a Recommendation challenge. For each rural planning area, rank its candidate road routes by the strength and timing of their recorded selection for an upgrade proposal. A useful model must learn transferable preferences from route shape, settlement access, facility access, local road connectivity, and planning- area context; complete planning areas, rather than individual roads, are held out. Every candidate has a 48-position ordered sequence with 14 features and a vector of 17 static features. The representation is anonymized: IDs are opaque, absolute coordinates and place names are absent, and coordinate direction is canonicalized. The training target score is graded: 3 denotes the strongest/earliest recorded selection evidence, 2 an intermediate selection period, 1 a later selection, and 0 no matched recorded selection. Rank candidates independently within each query_id; scores are compared only inside the same query. The training set contains 56,128 candidates in 472 queries. The test set contains 14,860 candidates in 122 unseen queries. Every query has at least 24 and at most 192 candidates and at least one relevant candidate. Evaluation Submissions are evaluated by the Rural Road Ranking Score, a query-macro average of three complementary ranking components. For each query: NDCG@10 uses graded gain 2^score - 1 and logarithmic rank discount. Binary average precision treats target levels 1, 2, and 3 as positive. Graded pairwise concordance is the fraction of candidate pairs with different target score whose predicted order is correct; a predicted tie receives half credit. The raw query score is: 0.55 × NDCG@10 + 0.25 × average_precision + 0.20 × graded_concordance A prediction vector that is constant within an evaluable query receives zero for that query. Candidate-ID text is used only as a deterministic tie breaker after predicted score. The final result is the direct, uncalibrated mean of the query scores. The platform may evaluate a row shard that contains only one target level from a query. Such a fragment has no correct or incorrect within-query ordering, so it is omitted from the macro mean. If a shard contains no evaluable query at all, every ordering is equivalent and the score is 1.0. This shard rule does not affect the full evaluation set, where every query contains positive and negative candidates. Direction: Maximize Minimum score: 0.0 Maximum score: 1.0 An exactly target-ordered submission scores 1.0. Dataset All solver-visible files are under dataset/public/ when the solution runs. | File | Contents | |---|---| | train.csv | Training candidate metadata and labels; 56,128 rows. | | test.csv | Unlabeled held-out candidates; 14,860 rows. | | train_sequence.npy | float16 array with shape (56128, 48, 14). | | test_sequence.npy | float16 array with shape (14860, 48, 14). | | train_static.npy | float32 array with shape (56128, 17). | | test_static.npy | float32 array with shape (14860, 17). | | FEATURE_SCHEMA.json | Ordered feature names, shapes, dtypes, and alignment rule. | | sample_submission.csv | Complete syntactically valid example using seeded random priorities. | CSV columns | File / column | Type | Meaning | |---|---|---| | train.csv: query_id | string | Opaque planning-area query token. | | train.csv: candidate_id | string | Opaque grouped road-candidate token, unique across the release. | | train.csv: tensor_row | int64 | Zero-based row in train_sequence.npy and train_static.npy. | | train.csv: score | int64 | Graded training target in {0,1,2,3}. | | test.csv: query_id | string | Opaque held-out planning-area query token. | | test.csv: candidate_id | string | Opaque grouped test road-candidate token. | | test.csv: tensor_row | int64 | Zero-based row in test_sequence.npy and test_static.npy. | The NPY arrays are aligned by tensor_row, not by token sorting. Sequence position follows normalized distance from one canonical endpoint to the other. Features are already finite and scaled but are not standardized to zero mean/unit variance. FEATURE_SCHEMA.json is authoritative for feature order and definitions. The split is deterministic and group-disjoint at the complete-query level. No query_id or candidate_id appears in both train and test. The held-out areas may have distributions unlike individual training areas, so random row validation is misleading; use query-grouped validation. Submission Submit a CSV with exactly these columns, in this order: | Column | Type | Meaning | |---|---|---| | candidate_id | string | A test candidate ID. | | score | finite numeric | Any real-valued priority; higher means rank earlier. | The file must contain exactly 14,860 rows, one per test candidate ID. Row order is irrelevant because grading aligns by candidate_id. Missing, extra, null, or duplicate IDs; missing/extra/reordered columns; and non-numeric, null, or infinite scores are invalid and raise a grading error. Example: candidate_id,score c_0123456789ab_0123456789abcdef01,0.731 c_0123456789ab_fedcba9876543210fe,-0.204 Constraints Use only the supplied public files and libraries already available in the execution image. The solution must run offline: no internet calls, hosted APIs, external datasets, source-record lookup, or challenge-specific pretrained checkpoints. Public general-purpose package weights may be used only when they are already available locally and work offline. The selected compute tier is A10G — Standard ML workloads. The trained model must materially use CUDA; CPU loading and feature engineering are allowed. Any offline GPU-accelerated modeling approach is permitted. Choose the validation design, feature representation, objective, architecture, and ensembling strategy from the supplied data rather than following a prescribed baseline. Write the final submission to ./working/submission.csv. &nbsp;
